# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS EVEN THOUGH NOTHING IS WRONG TODAY. The audit in
# antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197 went looking for required-check lists that
# had drifted away from the checks CI actually produces. This repository's list
# had not: the six names below are exactly the six check-runs the latest main
# commit produced, and exactly the six the latest pull_request check-suite
# produced, all `success`. Nothing needed correcting. It is declared anyway,
# because the list currently lives only in the GitHub settings page, and that
# page has no diff, no reviewer and no history anyone reads. The next job added
# to ci.yml will not appear in it, and the gap between "CI runs it" and "a PR
# cannot merge without it" is invisible from both ends. The sibling
# rust-img-qcow2 is what that looks like after the fact: it produces a sixth
# check, `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`, that nobody ever made required, and
# the only reason we know is that somebody went and counted. Here the counting
# is committed, so the next person can read it instead of repeating it.
#
# WHAT EACH NAME IS. All six come from .github/workflows/ci.yml, which triggers
# on `pull_request`, so all six can gate a pull request — a check that only runs
# on a tag or a schedule must never be declared, because a required check that
# never reports blocks the branch permanently. release.yml is exactly that and
# is deliberately absent from this list.
#
#   test / ubuntu-latest    the `test` job's matrix legs. Debug profile, so
#   test / macos-latest     overflow checks are on; this is the run that carries
#   test / windows-latest   the EXPECT_OVERFLOW_CHECKS handshake that
#                           tests/ci_profile.rs insists on. Three platforms
#                           because the byte-level VHDX code is the kind that
#                           differs by target, and windows-latest is the leg
#                           most likely to be quietly dropped.
#   test (release)          the release-profile run. Not redundant beside the
#                           matrix: release lets an integer overflow wrap where
#                           debug panics, so a test guarding a wrapping
#                           computation passes the debug run for the wrong
#                           reason. See the long comment above the job.
#   qemu-validation         cross-validates our reader/writer against
#                           `qemu-img`. The only job that proves we agree with
#                           an implementation we did not write.
#   fmt                     `cargo fmt --check`.
#
# TWO JOB-NAMING SHAPES IN ONE FILE, AND THE SPELLINGS ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.
# GitHub reports a check-run under the job's rendered `name:`, and ci.yml uses
# two different constructions:
#
#   name: test / ${{ matrix.os }}   ->  "test / ubuntu-latest"   (our own text,
#                                       spaces around the slash because we typed
#                                       them)
#   test-release:  name: test (release)  ->  "test (release)"    (our own text
#                                       again; the parentheses are literal, and
#                                       there is NO slash)
#
# Neither is a GitHub convention — both are just the strings in `name:` — which
# is why they cannot be inferred from each other. Writing `test / release`, or
# `test (ubuntu-latest)`, or dropping a space to `test/ubuntu-latest`, produces
# a name nothing will ever report. With enforce_admins on, that is a repository
# nobody can merge into. If a job is renamed, this file changes in the same
# commit; github-guard will keep the old name required until the new one has
# been green on main once, so the rename does not open a window.
[checks]
	required = fmt
	required = qemu-validation
	required = test / ubuntu-latest
	required = test / macos-latest
	required = test / windows-latest
	required = test (release)
